ents' discards and melds.\n\nReason: Looking at valid actions: Play W2, T8, T2, T6, T1, T7, W4, B2.\n- Discarding a W2 would break a potential pung, which is valuable for scoring (Pung of Terminals or Honors? W2 is simple, not terminal/honor). But keeping three W2 offers flexibility: can be used as a pung or part of a Chow (W1-W2-W3 or W2-W3-W4). However, W1 and W3 counts are low (W1:1 left, W3:3 left), making Chow less likely.\n- Discarding T1 (terminal) is safe because T1 has 4 left unshown, but terminals are harder to use for simples hand and might help opponents? But T1 is a terminal, and my hand might aim for All Simples, so discarding T1 is good. However, T1 can form Chow T1-T2-T3; I have T1 and two T2, so if I draw T3, I could have a Chow. T3 has 2 left, possible.\n- Discarding T8: T8 has 4 left, but T8 can form sequences with T6-T7-T8 or T7-T8-T9. I have T6, T7, T8, so discarding T8 breaks a potential Chow T6-T7-T8. But T9 is zero left, so T7-T8-T9 impossible. T6-T7-T8 is possible if I keep all.\n- Discarding T6 or T7 similarly breaks potential sequences.\n- Discarding B2: I only have one B2, and B suit is sparse, likely not contributing much.\n- Discarding W4: I have one W4 in hand, and W4 is in my exposed meld, so redundant. But W4 can form Chow with W2-W3-W4 or W4-W5-W6 (already used). With W2 and W3, possible but W3 availability okay.\n\nBest discard likely T1 because it's a terminal, reduces complexity for All Simples, and keeps my W2 pung potential and sequence potential with T2/T6/T7/T8. Also, opponents haven't shown much interest in T1 (next player played T3, T4; across played T4, T5, T6, T7, T3; previous played T4, T5, T9, T6). T1 might be relatively safe.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
