Wind).  \n- Previous player has pung T1 and Chow W6-W7-W8.  \n\nMany W tiles are in play, but W2 is still somewhat available (3 unshown). Tiles like T5, T6, T7 have moderate availability.  \n\nReason: Keeping W2 triplet offers a concealed pung, which could contribute to \"Two Concealed Pungs\" (2 points) or \"Concealed Hand\" (4 points) if I win by self-draw. Also, with a W2 pung, I could aim for \"All Pungs\" (6 points) if I can form other pungs. My hand currently has W2 triplet, T2 pair, and singles. Discarding a less useful single like B2 or T5 might retain more flexibility. However, B2 is a single and not easily used in sequences given the low Dot availability (B1, B3, B4 are scarce). T5 is a middle Bamboo, but with T6 and T7, I could form sequences if I draw T4 or T8. But discarding T5 breaks that potential.  \n\nBetter to discard **B2** as it's isolated and less likely to form high-value patterns. This keeps my Bamboo sequence potential (T5-T6-T7) and my W2 pung possibility. Also, discarding B2 avoids giving away a potentially useful tile for opponents.  \n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
